    (v_topic_id, 'A 9-year-old falls from a trampoline and sustains a significantly displaced extension-type supracondylar humerus fracture. Upon examination, the child is unable to make an ''OK'' sign (unable to flex the IP joint of the thumb and DIP joint of the index finger). Which nerve is most likely injured?', '["Anterior interosseous nerve (AIN)", "Axillary nerve", "Musculocutaneous nerve", "Radial nerve", "Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The anterior interosseous nerve (AIN), a branch of the median nerve, is the most commonly injured nerve in extension-type supracondylar humerus fractures. Injury manifests as an inability to flex the IP joint of the thumb (flexor pollicis longus) and the DIP of the index finger (flexor digitorum profundus), resulting in an inability to make the ''''A-OK'''' sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-week-old infant, born breech, presents for a routine well-child check. Examination reveals asymmetric thigh skin folds and a positive Ortolani maneuver on the left. What is the most appropriate initial imaging modality to confirm the diagnosis?', '["CT scan of the pelvis", "Bone scan", "AP radiograph of the pelvis", "Ultrasound of the hips", "MRI of the hips"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) is most accurately assessed with ultrasound in infants under 4-6 months of age because the femoral head and acetabulum are predominantly cartilaginous and not fully visible on radiographs. A positive Ortolani test (reduction of a dislocated hip) strongly suggests DDH.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 16-year-old female gymnast complains of insidious onset, mechanical low back pain that is exacerbated by extension activities (e.g., back walkovers). Neurologic exam is normal. Oblique radiographs of the lumbar spine demonstrate a ''collar'' on the ''Scotty dog''. What is the specific anatomic defect in this condition?', '["Defect in the pars interarticularis", "Congenital failure of segmentation of the lumbar vertebrae", "Fracture of the transverse process", "Defect in the vertebral body endplate (Schmorl node)", "Herniation of the nucleus pulposus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Spondylolysis is a stress fracture or defect of the pars interarticularis. It is highly prevalent in adolescent athletes participating in sports involving repetitive lumbar hyperextension. On oblique radiographs, a radiolucent gap in the pars resembles a collar on the neck of the ''''Scotty dog''''.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 10-year-old basketball player presents with posterior heel pain. The pain worsens after running and improves with rest. Examination reveals tenderness upon medial-lateral compression of the calcaneal tuberosity (positive ''squeeze test''). There is tightness of the Achilles tendon. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Achilles tendon rupture", "Plantar fasciitis", "Calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)", "Tarsal coalition", "Calcaneal stress fracture"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sever disease is a traction apophysitis of the calcaneus where the Achilles tendon inserts. It is a common cause of heel pain in athletic children aged 8-12. The classic exam finding is pain with medial/lateral compression of the heel. Treatment includes heel cups, calf stretching, and NSAIDs.'),
