    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for unilateral right clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Equinus", "Supination", "Varus", "Adductus", "Cavus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 4-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for unilateral left clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Supination", "Cavus", "Varus", "Adductus", "Equinus"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #98592: A 9-month-old infant is referred to orthopedics for asymmetric gluteal folds and a leg length discrepancy. Which imaging modality is considered the gold standard to evaluate for Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) at this specific age?', '["Ultrasound of the hips", "MRI of the pelvis", "Bone scan", "CT scan", "AP radiograph of the pelvis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 13-year-old basketball player presents with posterior heel pain. The pain worsens after running and improves with rest. Examination reveals tenderness upon medial-lateral compression of the calcaneal tuberosity (positive ''squeeze test''). There is tightness of the Achilles tendon. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Plantar fasciitis", "Tarsal coalition", "Achilles tendon rupture", "Calcaneal stress fracture", "Calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Sever disease is a traction apophysitis of the calcaneus where the Achilles tendon inserts. It is a common cause of heel pain in athletic children aged 8-12. The classic exam finding is pain with medial/lateral compression of the heel. Treatment includes heel cups, calf stretching, and NSAIDs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 11-year-old track and field player presents with posterior heel pain. The pain worsens after running and improves with rest. Examination reveals tenderness upon medial-lateral compression of the calcaneal tuberosity (positive ''squeeze test''). There is tightness of the Achilles tendon. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Tarsal coalition", "Plantar fasciitis", "Achilles tendon rupture", "Calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)", "Calcaneal stress fracture"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sever disease is a traction apophysitis of the calcaneus where the Achilles tendon inserts. It is a common cause of heel pain in athletic children aged 8-12. The classic exam finding is pain with medial/lateral compression of the heel. Treatment includes heel cups, calf stretching, and NSAIDs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 11-year-old obese male presents with left groin and knee pain. He walks with a limp. On examination, when his left hip is passively flexed, it obligatorily goes into external rotation. Radiographs show a posterior and inferior displacement of the proximal femoral epiphysis. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Immediate non-weight bearing and in situ percutaneous pinning", "Pavlik harness", "Open reduction and internal fixation", "Closed reduction and spica casting", "Physical therapy and weight loss"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE) occurs typically in overweight adolescents. The hallmark physical exam finding is obligatory external rotation with hip flexion. Treatment is strictly in situ pinning (without attempts at forceful closed reduction, which increases the risk of avascular necrosis) to stabilize the physis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 10-year-old gymnastics player presents with posterior heel pain. The pain worsens after running and improves with rest. Examination reveals tenderness upon medial-lateral compression of the calcaneal tuberosity (positive ''squeeze test''). There is tightness of the Achilles tendon. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Plantar fasciitis", "Calcaneal stress fracture", "Tarsal coalition", "Calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)", "Achilles tendon rupture"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sever disease is a traction apophysitis of the calcaneus where the Achilles tendon inserts. It is a common cause of heel pain in athletic children aged 8-12. The classic exam finding is pain with medial/lateral compression of the heel. Treatment includes heel cups, calf stretching, and NSAIDs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #95210: A 8-month-old infant is referred to orthopedics for asymmetric gluteal folds and a leg length discrepancy. Which imaging modality is considered the gold standard to evaluate for Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) at this specific age?', '["MRI of the pelvis", "CT scan", "Bone scan", "Ultrasound of the hips", "AP radiograph of the pelvis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.');
